WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.240
Before looking for weaknesses, the assessor needs to understand what the application is supposed to do.

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:17.780
That includes the business purpose, major workflows, user roles, data types, authentication methods, authorization boundaries, APIs, integrations, and administrative functions.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.680
Many web application issues are visible only when the tester understands how the application is intended to behave.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.540
AI can help organize this discovery phase.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:34.840
It can summarize notes from kickoff meetings, turn application documentation into a role and workflow map, or group questions for developers.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:45.540
It can also help identify places where the assessor lacks enough context, such as unclear ownership of a workflow, ambiguous data sensitivity, or missing API documentation.

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.200
Authentication and authorization deserve careful attention.

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.740
Authentication answers who the user is.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.840
Authorization answers what that user is allowed to do.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:01:00.900
AI can explain the difference and help structure review notes, but it cannot confirm the boundary by itself.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.680
The assessor still needs evidence from application behavior, documentation, code review, or authorized testing.

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:12.140
Third-party integrations and APIs often expand the assessment surface.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:21.800
Payment providers, identity platforms, storage services, analytics tools, messaging systems, and partner APIs may all affect security.

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:26.500
AI can help maintain an architecture summary and a list of integration questions.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:32.400
The outcome should be a clear mental model of the application, not a pile of disconnected observations.
